## What this covers

Everything in `site/` that is read rather than run, and everything in `doc/`:
the publications, the pattern catalogue, the entries, the figures, the poster,
and the prose of the pages that carry them.

The code is separately licensed — see `LICENSE` (MIT). That covers `scripts/`
and the JavaScript and CSS embedded in the pages. Where the two meet, in a file
that is both a program and a publication, take the code under MIT and the words
under CC BY.

## What you may do

Share it and adapt it, for any purpose including commercially, provided you
give appropriate credit, link to the licence, and say if you changed anything.

Quote a pattern in a design document. Put an entry on a wall. Teach from it.
Fork the catalogue and disagree with it in public. Translate it. Print the
poster. None of that needs permission.

## Attribution

Credit "Organon Mind" and link to https://organonmind.org where practical. A
citation of a specific pattern is best made against its number and its paper —
for example *Honest Gauge (OM-001, 12)* — because a number here is permanent
and is never reused.

## Why this licence

The programme's stated position is that a pattern must be refusable, and that a
language argued in public gets corrected faster than one held back until it is
safe. A licence that stopped people using these patterns at work would bar the
only audience able to refuse them on evidence.
